#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f638f4-f86a-515f-9f34-79bab5aed9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={28}^{∘},{132}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253720} {:answer/id #uuid "224f6339-4d6f-5e84-b12e-6e6df9f718ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={52}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253977} {:answer/id #uuid "8a43384c-d861-5f3a-b84c-c613ddd7827e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={32}^{∘},{128}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254892} {:answer/id #uuid "a13e94d6-c61b-5c3f-aae3-300caad1d7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{158}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255089} {:answer/id #uuid "d940938c-d2de-57cd-9c70-133a3360d2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d940938c-d2de-57cd-9c70-133a3360d2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255594} :answer-field/id #uuid "7908722b-df99-5603-8d9c-625d78a85041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "722c6645-d9eb-5787-9276-c39131ce1533" :question/math-academy-id "q-120828" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})=41.810{…}^{∘}≈{41.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${41.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc0c031fe6ccbb40dc77474582b301456f537ee2a649ba2d5b3bca9b1240618f.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{41.81}^{∘} \\\\  & ={138.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464e7f61-ecbe-5d92-a768-bdba96ba66de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={41}^{∘},{139}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254263} {:answer/id #uuid "69d72443-b15d-5420-872d-20cf241c18f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={59}^{∘},{121}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254622} {:answer/id #uuid "7e19217d-64a1-5640-bcbd-4834c9ad3d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={33}^{∘},{147}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254788} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2bbeef-4a86-5df0-80c1-4741f9633531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={49}^{∘},{131}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255037} {:answer/id #uuid "f21259fd-d614-5577-a3a5-f9fb03962578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f21259fd-d614-5577-a3a5-f9fb03962578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255785} :answer-field/id #uuid "9540c28b-d25c-5852-ad5d-ea5925808fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d378f08-b29e-5a1d-8989-f385e5bd482e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120835" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=38.682{…}^{∘}≈{38.68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${38.68}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/12/12b17e3994be5e45af3aca185b22618dd0ec7288fba18a4131387783323247a0.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{38.68}^{∘} \\\\  & ={141.32}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6148f0-c0ef-5ec8-84fa-96f7399895bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254084} {:answer/id #uuid "aa374c04-93de-5b00-a6c3-ee0239b5dbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255176} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec00b1-8b3d-578e-aa9d-b52b625b0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255422} {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} {:answer/id #uuid "f9df6008-3042-5fe7-bfd0-7945f4fd4d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ca042d-c28f-56ba-b846-2e285284fac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "814abbe2-963b-55aa-99fd-84de01c3f66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120893" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](images/49/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51288993-1186-54f7-b8f9-c3dedec828cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254373} {:answer/id #uuid "6636f841-f6b2-5a4a-8b18-128444b21885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254587} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ed8e9d-6c96-5472-ab47-f9b913bb9f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255152} {:answer/id #uuid "a96a54c4-bfd5-505c-91fe-009524c103c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255163} {:answer/id #uuid "eac5a2ac-906b-5eeb-80d9-346addcba2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eac5a2ac-906b-5eeb-80d9-346addcba2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53684b1-60b5-56d0-bc46-63f1c5c26e72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70af0da8-cc0a-5a44-abbf-395198d56bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120896" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{7}{9}) & =2.461\\,918…≈2.461\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.461\\,9$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.461\\,9.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.461\\,9 & ≈0.679\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.679\\,7.$\n\n![](images/ea/eafc9be7094c9c0ccdf167cc737d494efec40014a89ad793f7ed34ea5eed94ae.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.679\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.821\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.461\\,9\\cdot 3.821\\,3≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111eb72f-77db-56c0-809e-6b4a599df81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398216} {:answer/id #uuid "197ae8e0-022c-5701-9bc2-270d31cca02d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398217} {:answer/id #uuid "d99dcb4d-2a83-549a-b986-4a60429f6baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398218} {:answer/id #uuid "631461f4-e54f-5325-b27b-475e158a4548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398219} {:answer/id #uuid "98436ba7-22e8-5883-92bc-f2a392e4c571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197ae8e0-022c-5701-9bc2-270d31cca02d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398217} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d0bc62-23d7-51e0-a40e-82b353c65411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13b68e5e-22d6-5fa0-a4a4-6c5d1f4c0a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-120911" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=1\\cdot \\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $2+2\\sqrt{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2+2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1beb1460-5a9d-5c09-9b3b-2e0b4ddaf401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253904} {:answer/id #uuid "36da961c-7070-5c7e-a1f8-392f2b069620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254142} {:answer/id #uuid "517e430a-634f-5708-9d27-1aa7f50f0988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254376} {:answer/id #uuid "86b231eb-df60-5b58-86ea-0c5183114af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254863} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d5d610-6f81-55d4-94c4-7726640fd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1beb1460-5a9d-5c09-9b3b-2e0b4ddaf401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253904} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd05a9-c8e1-5aa9-a21e-478096d5daed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4031e085-738d-5b9f-898b-4694f1a9c939" :question/math-academy-id "q-120940" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1b4c3b-0872-5484-846b-7c31f075326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d66023e-f256-5b13-916d-42dcaa6c013c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254497} {:answer/id #uuid "791eb4cd-5e49-57d9-b7e6-a52a40c5e041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254743} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} {:answer/id #uuid "be503456-5109-5ce6-90f6-46f497a9e83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee3993dd-3a89-5d8f-8088-b70e6d9fbfde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1acd4d45-a455-5e34-97cf-5720edb8c513" :question/math-academy-id "q-120981" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](images/23/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10153938-e8ec-5c96-b3c4-f24b6f2c9e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378465} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0d871b-1e3d-5943-b6dc-37263b8e5b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378466} {:answer/id #uuid "7435ff29-520c-5b90-b4ab-e7a36626dd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378467} {:answer/id #uuid "a5811c64-f904-57db-8d90-bc17655f40dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378468} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3aa1df-797b-5a4e-a99e-2440d295c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc3aa1df-797b-5a4e-a99e-2440d295c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378469} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b841300-5c7d-509b-8ab7-625e31f6a516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "261770c7-7955-5658-985f-a98194f9a404" :question/math-academy-id "q-120990" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=2t-\\sqrt{t}.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=9?$" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}(2t-\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={({t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{9} \\\\  & =[(9)^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}(9)^{3/2}]-[(0)^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =81-18-0 \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=9$ is $63.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4855a8b7-7a6b-58fd-bfcb-512c8d36c92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254289} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddb2256-cdec-5fd5-b64a-ffbc48c96995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f2399-b1ef-59c8-be8b-f72934d61b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255283} {:answer/id #uuid "ef612402-f1a0-55cb-8215-9500ae958c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255762} {:answer/id #uuid "faccb7a5-43af-5b0a-8c12-37d5995f9f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ddb2256-cdec-5fd5-b64a-ffbc48c96995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340} :answer-field/id #uuid "73fd2e63-bc3b-516e-aa50-4cef260983d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ad853df-f7ff-5b1c-95e8-898b94e2247f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120998" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t+1}.$ At time $t=0.5$ the particle is located at the position $x={e}^{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=0.5$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0.5,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(0.5)+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & ={e}^{2}+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+1}]}_{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(2)+1}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(0.5)+1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97786302-e865-5581-b08e-8da7c6b7a01b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4658f5ed-bea8-55d3-a5c8-e35701a6a2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254264} {:answer/id #uuid "98577715-7654-563c-bb0e-d13248802bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255018} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b96a5b-8ec0-5a2c-b97f-ec596610936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255310} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eeb10e-7c5a-5705-a0a6-9546f983b82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255585} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cee127-4c04-5f38-b790-e5f2e404fedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b96a5b-8ec0-5a2c-b97f-ec596610936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255310} :answer-field/id #uuid "82979c27-ebd4-5fae-902f-a919bb5b2e12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6a9809a-4ab2-58a8-8350-cebc2231a946" :question/math-academy-id "q-121066" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=-\\frac{1}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c7ab50-5a8b-5bcd-8483-cec8bf6bd3ce" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a65183c-2bf9-5a8f-ab7a-90a1855419e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253750} {:answer/id #uuid "13ce42f3-cac8-5bb4-9066-effdabcf2334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={63}^{∘},{243}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253840} {:answer/id #uuid "38b6122c-c8d7-5ed6-b92c-eefe16988d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={76}^{∘},{256}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254163} {:answer/id #uuid "748b63c3-6f43-569d-9e36-9b9a22a9eb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={70}^{∘},{250}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254700} {:answer/id #uuid "96a15fbc-f4a7-5fd5-b3c5-d8d3244e644a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={88}^{∘},{268}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a65183c-2bf9-5a8f-ab7a-90a1855419e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253750} :answer-field/id #uuid "409bc980-f28a-5a07-b298-2291184f8da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1252830-b58d-56d0-beaa-bb7162765f44" :question/math-academy-id "q-121127" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=8$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)=82.874\\,983{…}^{∘}≈{82.87}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${82.87}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={82.87}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{82.87}^{∘}={262.87}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0737c6dd-2968-5cc9-a8f8-c0079a8268a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253724} {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} {:answer/id #uuid "75bbd111-a6df-57f9-ac2f-45af1364cecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254712} {:answer/id #uuid "8398ba0c-a692-519d-9e74-4243e928998b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254841} {:answer/id #uuid "acc2456f-4e14-5655-aa35-0e7518c63750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7037b1e7-eb89-5813-ae66-13b1ef312f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/math-academy-id "q-121221" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129218 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9540b1c6-1f60-5074-bc72-614497b1d511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126021} {:answer/id #uuid "225ef1cc-e1